\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\ul\b\f0\fs24\'b6 Verse 1\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 1. One who is all unfit to count
\par As scholar in Thy school,
\par Thou of Thy love hast named a friend-
\par O kindness wonderful!
\par 
\par \cf1\ul\b\'b6 Verse 2\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 2. So weak am I, O gracious Lord,
\par So all unworthy Thee,
\par That e'en the dust upon Thy feet
\par Outweighs me utterly.
\par 
\par \cf1\ul\b\'b6 Verse 3\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 3. Thou dwellest in unshadowed light,
\par All sin and shame above-
\par That Thou shouldst bear our sin and shame,
\par How can I tell such love?
\par 
\par \cf1\ul\b\'b6 Verse 4\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 4. Ah, did not He the heavenly throne
\par A little thing esteem,
\par And did unworthy for my sake
\par A mortal body deem?
\par 
\par \cf1\ul\b\'b6 Verse 5\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 5. When in His flesh they drove the nails,
\par Did He not all endure?
\par What name is there to fit a life
\par So patient and so pure?
\par 
\par \cf1\ul\b\'b6 Verse 6\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 6. So, Love itself in human form,
\par For love of me He came;
\par I cannot look upon His face
\par For shame, for bitter shame.
\par 
\par \cf1\ul\b\'b6 Verse 7\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 7. If there is aught of worth in me,
\par It comes from Thee alone;
\par Then keep me safe, for so, O Lord,
\par Thou keepest but Thine own.\f1 
\par \cf1\ul\b 
\par }

